Reed text editor
================

Start Reed with reed for an unnamed buffer or reed FILE to edit a file. A
missing file is not created until a successful save. Use reed --help for
quick invocation syntax. Reed is modal and uses the alternate screen while
editing.

Modes and navigation
--------------------

Reed has Normal, Insert, Selection, Command, and Search modes. Positions and
edits are UTF-8 scalar boundaries. The cursor is an insertion boundary.

Normal mode:

  h / Left, l / Right       Move one scalar.
  j / Down, k / Up          Move one visual row when wrapping, otherwise one
                            logical line; retain the preferred cell column.
  b / n                     Move to the final scalar of the previous or
                            current/next word, respectively.
  B / N                     Move to the beginning of the current/previous
                            word, or skip to the next word beginning.
  a / Home, e / End         Move to logical line start/end boundary.
  A / E                     Move to line start/end and enter Insert mode.
  g / Ctrl+Home, G / Ctrl+End
                            Move to document start/end.
  [ / ]                     Move to previous/next paragraph.
  Page Up / Page Down       Move by a visible text viewport.
  i                         Enter Insert mode at the cursor.
  o / O                     Open a blank logical line below/above and enter
                            Insert mode on it.
  s / S / Alt+S             Start character selection, line selection, or
                            select the whole document.
  d / Backspace / Delete    Delete at cursor, before cursor, or at cursor.
  u / Shift+U                Undo / redo.
  c / x + motion            Copy / cut a motion range through clipd.
  cc / xx                   Copy / cut the complete logical line.
  v                         Paste at the cursor.
  / / ?                     Open command / incremental search prompt.

Words are runs of ASCII letters, digits, underscore, and valid non-ASCII
scalars. ASCII punctuation, whitespace, and line breaks separate words. b
and n land on the last scalar of their target word. B and N move to word
starts. Motions do not wrap at document boundaries. B from inside a word
moves to its start; another B at that start continues to the previous word.
N skips the word under the cursor and goes to the next word start.

Paragraphs are runs of nonblank logical lines. Empty lines and lines containing
only ASCII spaces, tabs, or carriage returns separate paragraphs. [ from
inside a paragraph moves to its first line; from that line's beginning or a
separator it moves to the previous nonblank paragraph. ] moves to the next
nonblank paragraph. At document edges, motions stay in place. { and } are
reserved and currently do nothing.

Selection
---------

s starts character selection, S starts line selection, and Alt+S selects
the document. Navigation extends from the original anchor. Pressing the active
selection key cancels; pressing the other changes granularity while keeping
the anchor and endpoint. Escape cancels at the current endpoint. Line selection
includes complete logical lines and appropriate newline bytes without
inventing a final newline. d deletes a selection as one undoable edit and
returns to Normal mode. i does not enter Insert mode from selection.

The system clipboard is shared within your authenticated session. In selection,
c copies, x cuts, and v replaces the selection. In Normal mode, c/x take one
of b/B/n/N, a/e, g/G, [ or ] as a suffix; cc/xx use the complete logical line
under the selection newline rule. Escape cancels a pending operator. Word-end
ranges include the destination scalar even though edits use half-open ranges.
Copy leaves the cursor fixed; successful selection operations return to Normal.

Cut publishes successfully before deleting. Paste downloads a complete item
before one undoable replacement. Failure leaves text, selection and undo/redo
unchanged. Empty text deletes a selection or does nothing without one. NUL
bytes and pastes exceeding Reed's 2 MiB document capacity are rejected. No
truncation occurs. Undo/redo, typing and ordinary deletion do not change the
clipboard. Contents survive the source editor exiting; logout or restarting
clipd loses them. There is no private yank register or clipboard history.

Insert, commands, and search
---------------------------

Insert mode accepts text, arrow navigation, Home/End, Backspace, Delete, and
Enter for a newline. Shift+Arrow does not select. Escape returns to Normal
mode. The o and O commands work in empty files, at the first or last line,
and when the file has no trailing newline. Undo restores the previous line and
final-newline state.

/ opens Reed's command prompt. The command name ends at a space or tab and is
case-insensitive, but must match exactly. Tab completes an unambiguous command
name; it does not complete paths.

  /s             Save the current file.
  /q             Quit with unsaved-change protection.
  /sq            Save, then quit only if saving succeeds.
  /saveas PATH   Save as a new path; the destination must not exist.
  /open PATH     Open another file.
  /find TEXT     Find literal text forward, wrapping to the document start.

Command errors appear in the status row. Failed or cancelled actions retain
the buffer. Alt+S means Select All, not Save.

? opens incremental literal search. All matches highlight while the query is
edited, with Backspace/Delete editing the query. Escape cancels and returns to
the original cursor. Enter selects the match nearest the original cursor
(ties choose the later match), places the cursor after it, and enters Search
mode. No match returns to Normal mode at the original cursor. In Search mode,
n advances to the next match and b to the previous match. Search navigation
uses the current cursor, does not wrap, and warns while leaving the cursor
unchanged when no match exists. ? or Escape exits Search mode at the current
cursor and clears highlights. Editing is unavailable until Search mode ends.

Display and text limits
----------------------

Soft wrapping is the default. Long logical lines occupy additional visual rows
without adding newline bytes. j/k and arrows move by visual row when wrapping
is enabled, while a/e, Home/End, and paragraph motions remain logical. Tabs
expand to the configured cell stop but remain tab bytes in the file. Setting
wrap = false disables wrapping and uses one shared horizontal offset for all
lines; the status row and prompts remain stationary.

Reed accepts valid UTF-8 text and rejects NUL or malformed UTF-8 in files. The
cursor and renderer operate on Unicode scalar boundaries and count each scalar
as one cell. Editing is not grapheme-aware and does not account for combining
marks or wide-script display width. Search is literal, not regular-expression
based. Files are limited to 2 MiB and paths to 255 bytes. Undo and redo retain
up to 64 snapshots and 8 MiB per stack.

Configuration
-------------

Reed reads its optional configuration at startup from:

    ~/.config/reed/config

It does not create the directories or file and does not reload settings during
an editing session. Missing configuration uses these defaults:

    wrap = true
    tab_width = 4

To configure Reed, use jump to go to your home directory, create each
missing parent directory with mkdir, then open the new file with Reed, enter
the settings, and save with /s. For example:

    jump
    mkdir .config
    mkdir .config/reed
    reed .config/reed/config

wrap accepts true or false. tab_width accepts an integer from 1 through
16. Blank lines and whitespace around assignments are allowed. Lines may use
#, ;, or // comments. Duplicate or unknown keys, malformed values, NUL,
lines longer than 127 bytes, and files larger than 1023 bytes invalidate the
whole file. Reed keeps its built-in defaults and displays a warning; it never
partially applies invalid settings or rewrites the file.

Reed has no separate history, state, or cache file. It saves only the path
chosen by the user. Saving an existing file uses a conditional replacement
against its opened generation; a stale save is rejected and offers cancel,
Save As, or explicit overwrite after reading a complete new version. Save As
never overwrites an existing destination. Runtime conflict handling does not
promise crash atomicity or power-loss durability.
